  "title": "observability signals instrumentation and service objectives",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Observability is the ability to infer internal system behavior from emitted telemetry. Metrics summarize measurements, logs record events, and traces connect work across boundaries; one signal rarely answers every diagnostic question.",
    "Instrumentation creates telemetry; collection, processing, transport, storage, query, visualization, and alerting are separate pipeline stages with distinct failure and cost modes.",
    "OpenTelemetry defines vendor-neutral APIs, SDKs, semantic conventions, OTLP, and Collector components. Its signals have different specification maturity, so stable and experimental features must not be described as equivalent.",
    "A metric time series is identified by its metric name and label set. Unbounded labels create cardinality growth; histograms preserve distributions for quantile analysis, while averages can hide tail latency.",
    "An SLI measures a user-relevant property, an SLO sets its target over a window, and an error budget represents the tolerated unreliability. Alerts should lead to an actionable response rather than merely report a noisy condition.",
    "Telemetry can expose secrets and personal data. Sampling, retention, redaction, access controls, and cost limits are part of system design, not optional dashboard details."
  ],
